## Research Participants

The study was conducted using a Google Forms questionnaire completed by 14 participants from different university user groups. The participants included:


9 Students	(64.3%)
3 Non-Teaching Staff (21.4%)
1 Teaching Staff (7.1%)
1 Visitors	(7.1%)

Total	14	100%

## Evidence Collected

The research collected the following evidence:

Responses from a Google Forms questionnaire.
Participant demographic information.
Data on queue experiences at different university service points.
User opinions on virtual queue management.
Suggestions for improving the current queue system.
Key Evidence

## The research showed that:

Long queues are experienced across several university service points.
The Finance Office is the busiest service point, followed by the Food's Mess and Accommodation Office.
Most users prefer joining queues remotely through a mobile application.
Users want real-time queue updates, estimated waiting times, and notifications before their turn.
Participants from different user groups—not only students—expressed interest in using a virtual queue management system.
Research Outcome

The research provides clear evidence that a Virtual Queue Management Mobile Application would address real user needs by reducing waiting time, minimizing congestion, improving communication, and enhancing the overall user experience at university service points.
